<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic
    SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="QueryFlag: Create query templates" id="QueryFlag-Create-Templates">

  <primary-label ref="queryflag"/>
  <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

  <tldr>
    <p>Settings: <ui-path>Settings | Tools | Database | QueryFlag</ui-path></p>
    <p>From a console: select a query and choose <control>Add Query Flag</control> in the context menu</p>
    <p>Token: <code>&lt;QUERY_FLAG_TOKEN&gt;</code></p>
  </tldr>

  <link-summary>Define, edit and remove QueryFlag templates: a named query for a data source with a token for the selected text.</link-summary>
  <card-summary>Save a query for a data source with a token that the selected text replaces.</card-summary>
  <web-summary>Create QueryFlag query templates in JetBrains IDEs: add a token for the selected text, quote it correctly in SQL and MongoDB, and manage templates.</web-summary>

  <p>
    A template, which the QueryFlag settings call an entry, is a named query for one data source.
    The query contains the token <code>&lt;QUERY_FLAG_TOKEN&gt;</code>.
    When you <a href="Work-with-QueryFlag.topic">run the template</a>, QueryFlag replaces the token with the text you selected in the editor.
  </p>

  <chapter title="Before you begin" id="before-you-begin">
    <p>
      Add the data source that the query runs against in the <control>Database</control> tool window.
      The template dialog lists the data sources that the current project can use.
    </p>
  </chapter>

  <chapter title="Add a template in Settings" id="add-template">
    <procedure title="Add a template" id="add-template-procedure">
      <step>
        Press <shortcut>Ctrl+Alt+S</shortcut> (macOS: <shortcut>⌘ ,</shortcut>) to open the <control>Settings</control> dialog,
        and select <ui-path>Tools | Database | QueryFlag</ui-path>.
        <p>To find the page faster, type <code>QueryFlag</code> in the search field of the <control>Settings</control> dialog.</p>
        <!-- planned-image src="queryflag_settings_page.png" alt="QueryFlag settings page with three templates and the two options" -->
      </step>
      <step>
        Click <control>Add</control> (<control>+</control>) on the toolbar above the list, or press <shortcut>Alt+Insert</shortcut> (macOS: <shortcut>⌘ N</shortcut>).
        <p>When the list is empty, you can also click the <control>Add a QueryFlag Entry</control> link.</p>
      </step>
      <step>
        In the <control>Add a QueryFlag Entry</control> dialog, fill in the fields:
        <deflist type="medium">
          <def title="Name" id="field-name">
            The label of the template in the <control>Query Flag in DB</control> menu.
            The dialog suggests a combination of database, table and column name, for example <code>demo.customers.email</code>.
          </def>
          <def title="DataSource" id="field-datasource">
            The data source the query runs against.
            The list shows the data sources from the <control>Database</control> tool window.
          </def>
          <def title="QueryFlag with Token" id="field-query">
            The query, with at least one <code>&lt;QUERY_FLAG_TOKEN&gt;</code> token.
            The field offers syntax highlighting and code completion for the query language of the data source.
            See <a anchor="token-rules"/>.
          </def>
        </deflist>
        <!-- planned-image src="queryflag_add_template_dialog.png" alt="Add a QueryFlag Entry dialog with name, data source and a query containing the QUERY_FLAG_TOKEN placeholder" -->
      </step>
      <step>
        Click <control>OK</control>.
        If a field is empty or the query has no token, the dialog shows an error and stays open.
      </step>
      <step>
        Click <control>Apply</control> or <control>OK</control> in the <control>Settings</control> dialog to save the template.
      </step>
    </procedure>
    <p>
      The template now appears in the <control>Query Flag in DB</control> context menu of the editor.
    </p>
  </chapter>

  <chapter title="Write the template query" id="token-rules">
    <p>
      When you run a template, QueryFlag removes every double quote (<code>"</code>) from the selected text
      and puts the rest in place of each <code>&lt;QUERY_FLAG_TOKEN&gt;</code> token, exactly as it is.
      It does not add quotes and does not escape the value.
      Write the query so that it is valid after this replacement:
    </p>
    <list>
      <li>
        Put quotes around the token wherever the query needs a string literal:
        single quotes in SQL (<code>'&lt;QUERY_FLAG_TOKEN&gt;'</code>) and double quotes in MongoDB (<code>"&lt;QUERY_FLAG_TOKEN&gt;"</code>).
      </li>
      <li>Leave the token unquoted for numbers.</li>
      <li>Type the token exactly as shown, in upper case and with the angle brackets.</li>
      <li>Use the token as often as you need: QueryFlag replaces every occurrence.</li>
      <li>
        Lines that start with <code>//</code> are notes.
        QueryFlag removes them when it saves the template, so they never reach the database.
      </li>
    </list>

    <p>SQL examples:</p>
    <code-block lang="sql"><![CDATA[
-- Text value, partial match (the placeholder in the dialog uses this form)
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE email LIKE '%<QUERY_FLAG_TOKEN>%';

-- Text value, exact match
SELECT * FROM feature_flags WHERE key = '<QUERY_FLAG_TOKEN>';

-- Numeric value: no quotes
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE id = <QUERY_FLAG_TOKEN>;
]]></code-block>

    <p>MongoDB example:</p>
    <code-block lang="javascript"><![CDATA[
db.getCollection("customers").find({email: {$regex: "<QUERY_FLAG_TOKEN>"}})
]]></code-block>

    <note>
      Single quotes in the selected text are not escaped.
      If you select a value that contains a single quote, the SQL statement is invalid.
    </note>

    <tip>
      Syntax highlighting and completion in the query field follow the data source that is selected when the dialog opens.
      For a new template, that is the first data source of the project.
      Selecting another data source does not change the highlighting.
      To get highlighting for another database type, such as MongoDB, save the template and open it again with <control>Edit</control>,
      or <a anchor="create-from-console">create it from a console</a> of that data source.
    </tip>
  </chapter>

  <chapter title="Create a template from a database console" id="create-from-console">
    <p>
      If you already have a working query in a query console, turn it into a template.
    </p>
    <procedure title="Add a template from a query console" id="add-from-console-procedure">
      <step>
        In a query console of the data source, select the query.
      </step>
      <step>
        Right-click the selection and select <control>Add Query Flag</control>.
        <!-- planned-image src="queryflag_add_from_console_menu.png" alt="Database console context menu with Add Query Flag" -->
        <p>
          The <control>Add a QueryFlag Entry</control> dialog opens with these values:
          <control>Name</control> and <control>DataSource</control> are set to the console's data source,
          and <control>QueryFlag with Token</control> contains the selected text without surrounding spaces and surrounding double quotes.
        </p>
      </step>
      <step>
        Replace the value that should come from the editor selection with <code>&lt;QUERY_FLAG_TOKEN&gt;</code>.
        <p>
          If the selection had no token, QueryFlag adds the line
          <code>// Need to add at least one &lt;QUERY_FLAG_TOKEN&gt; replacement token</code>.
          Delete this line after you add the token.
          The line is removed on save anyway, and it does not count as a token in the saved query.
        </p>
      </step>
      <step>
        Change <control>Name</control> to a name you can recognize in the menu, and click <control>OK</control>.
      </step>
    </procedure>
    <p>
      QueryFlag saves the template immediately.
      You do not need to click <control>Apply</control> in Settings.
    </p>
    <note>
      <control>Add Query Flag</control> appears only in the context menu of a database console, and only when the selection is not blank.
    </note>
  </chapter>

  <chapter title="Edit or remove a template" id="edit-remove">
    <procedure title="Edit a template" id="edit-template">
      <step>
        In <ui-path>Settings | Tools | Database | QueryFlag</ui-path>, select the template in the list.
      </step>
      <step>
        Click <control>Edit</control> on the toolbar.
        The same dialog opens, with the title <control>Add a QueryFlag Entry</control>.
      </step>
      <step>
        Change the fields, click <control>OK</control>, and then click <control>Apply</control>.
      </step>
    </procedure>
    <procedure title="Remove a template" id="remove-template">
      <step>
        In <ui-path>Settings | Tools | Database | QueryFlag</ui-path>, select the template in the list.
      </step>
      <step>
        Click <control>Remove</control> (<control>-</control>) on the toolbar, and then click <control>Apply</control>.
      </step>
    </procedure>
  </chapter>

  <chapter title="Where templates are available" id="template-scope">
    <p>
      QueryFlag stores templates for the whole IDE, not per project.
      Every project shows the same list in Settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <control>Query Flag in DB</control> menu lists a template only in projects where its data source is available.
      In other projects, the settings list shows the template with the red text
      "DataSource not found, please check your Databases settings".
      See <a href="QueryFlag-Troubleshooting.topic" anchor="datasource-not-found"/>.
    </p>
  </chapter>

